          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Eitoku's screen, Chinese Lions … reveals the bold, brightly colored style of painting preferred by the samurai.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Japanese art",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_art",
          "accessed": "2026-11-27",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "In sharp contrast to the previous Muromachi period, the Azuchi–Momoyama period was characterized by a grandiose polychrome style, with extensive use of gold and silver foil that would be applied to paintings, garments, architecture, etc.; and by works on a very large scale.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Japanese painting",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_painting",
          "accessed": "2026-11-27",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Byōbu adorned samurai residences, conveying high rank and demonstrating wealth and power.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Byōbu",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/By%C5%8Dbu",
